<%# Floating sound on/off toggle for the draw sound (public/sounds/draw-sound.mp3),
  available to host and guest alike, on every game screen (waiting/active/
  finished) — the only place it's meaningful, so it's rendered from
  games/show.html.erb alone, never from pages/home or pages/enter.

  Entirely client-side: the preference lives in localStorage (see
  javascript/sound_preference.js), not on the server, so the server always
  renders the same "unmuted" markup below — the Stimulus controller
  corrects it to the real, persisted state on connect.

  data-turbo-permanent (paired with the stable #sound-toggle id) is load-
  bearing, not decorative: a broadcast_refresh_to morph (start/restart, see
  GamesController) re-renders the whole page in place, and Turbo's morph
  preserves an *existing* node rather than replacing it whenever it can —
  which means it patches this element's attributes to match the freshly
  rendered (always-"unmuted") markup above, in place, WITHOUT removing or
  re-adding the node. Stimulus only connects/disconnects controllers when
  an element is actually added to or removed from the DOM, so that in-place
  attribute patch never triggers a reconnect to fix it back up — a muted
  user's button would otherwise revert to showing "unmuted" after every
  single morph, permanently, until they happened to reload the page or
  toggle it again. data-turbo-permanent sidesteps the whole problem by
  telling Turbo to skip this element during morphing entirely, so the node,
  its attributes, and its live controller instance all survive untouched —
  the fix Turbo actually documents for exactly this "don't let a refresh
  clobber client-only UI state" case.

  draw_sound_controller.js reads the same preference directly whenever a
  ball is about to play, so this toggle never has to reach into that
  controller directly; the two only ever communicate through localStorage.

  Fixed bottom-right, clear of the ball/draw button/history chips above it
  and the board below — see test/system/sound_toggle_test.rb for the
  narrow-viewport regression coverage.

  Built with tag.button and hash attributes rather than a hand-written
  aria-label HTML attribute on purpose: the i18n hardcoded-string guard
  (see i18n_hardcoded_strings_test) regex-scans raw attribute text for the
  literal attribute name immediately followed by a quote, and a translated
  value's own embedded quotes trip it into capturing a false-positive
  "hardcoded" fragment. Every other translated attribute in this app
  (placeholder, alt, turbo_confirm...) already goes through Ruby hash
  syntax passed to a helper for the same reason — this follows that same,
  guard-compatible convention instead of introducing a new one.
%>
<% mute_label = t("games.show.sound.mute_label") %>
<% unmute_label = t("games.show.sound.unmute_label") %>
<%= tag.button type: "button",
      id: "sound-toggle",
      data: {
        controller: "sound-toggle",
        turbo_permanent: true,
        action: "click->sound-toggle#toggle",
        sound_toggle_mute_label_value: mute_label,
        sound_toggle_unmute_label_value: unmute_label
      },
      aria: { pressed: false, label: mute_label },
      class: "group fixed bottom-5 right-5 z-40 inline-flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full " \
             "border-[1.5px] shadow-lg transition cursor-pointer border-bingo-line bg-bingo-surface text-bingo-fg " \
             "hover:border-bingo-fg data-[state=muted]:border-bingo-accent data-[state=muted]:bg-bingo-accent " \
             "data-[state=muted]:text-bingo-on-accent data-[state=muted]:hover:brightness-125" do %>
  <span class="group-data-[state=muted]:hidden"><%= render "games/sound_on_icon" %></span>
  <span class="hidden group-data-[state=muted]:inline"><%= render "games/sound_off_icon" %></span>
<% end %>
